               CHNTSRCH: Undocumented features
  
  A few features are not documented in LMLO: Sources & Chants. Implemented recently, these
  features have not been used to the same extent and thus have not been subjected to
  extensive testing.
  
  While running, the program displays on screen three options with which the user may
  interrupt the program:
     if an x is pressed, the program ends (eXits) immediately;
     if a w is pressed, the program will Write the current data and summaries and end;
     if a b is pressed the display of these options will be replaced by a current breakdown of
       the summaries and the program will continue. The b may be pressed repeatedly. The x
       or w are still valid.
  /sb   using /sb on the command line causes this breakdown to be displayed and updated
          continuously.
  
  The program may also be interrupted as usual with Ctrl-C or Ctrl-Break.
  
  The x, w, and b commands that can be used to interrupt the program will be obvious to the
  user through the display on the screen. Unless the user reads this document, the command
  line parameter /sb and the other parameters described below will remain unknown.
  
  The first of these remaining features was designed to allow users to search for text without
  the undesirable consequences outlined in LMLO: Source & Chants, p. 208 under Output
  including letters. Designed to find matches within chantwords, the search is in fact much
  more flexible, looking at every element   "word"   delimited by spaces in every line of the
  data, limited only when other parameters such as /xm or /xs are in the command. Here are
  the details.
  /t this parameter allows the user to search for any string(s) in any line: the strings are
       specified in the /f parameter, using double delimiters, thus /f=":string1:string2:".
       Matches occur when ALL strings are found within a (chant)word delimited by spaces.
       The strings are sensitive to case.
     Thus, /t /f=":em:0:1:" initiates a search for any chantword that contains the letters
     em (together) and the pitches 0 and 1 (in any order). The command /t /f=":e:m:.0:1:"
     would initiate a search for any chantword that contains the letters e and m (in any
     order) and the pitch 0 immediately after a syllable separator and the pitch 1 (in any
     order).
  
  When the documented parameter /f="&" is used, only references are written to the output.
  The next parameter instructs the program to write the data even in this case.
  /wd   when used with /f="&" this parameter causes the data as well as the references to
          be written to the output.
  
  The documented parameter /xk= with a value limits the search to the season (Kalendar
  occasion) specified. 
  /xk   without a value, this parameter causes the season (Kalendar information) to be added
          to the summaries, but does not limit the search to a particular season.
  
  The last new parameter works only if certain codes are present in the data. These codes are
  placed between (z and a closing parenthesis ). The codes are intended to represent the
  different repertories and textual styles that occur in the data: the rhymed repertory (z @r);
  the Dominican repertory (z OP); a prose item identified by (z @p). If the user knows which
  codes are present in the data, the search may be limited to particular repertories by placing
  the code as a value for this parameter.
  /xr=  with a value, this parameter limits the search to the repertory specified, which is
          cued in the data between (z and ): e.g. (z @r OP).
  Even if this parameter is not used, the program notes each of these codes and at the end of
  the output summarizes the matches according to the different repertories found.
  
  
  